.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt BASE-ACCOUNTS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm base-accounts
.Nd create missing base-system groups and users
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl anqv
.Op Fl R Ar rootdir
.Op Fl r Ar refdir
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Pa /etc/group
and
.Pa /etc/master.passwd
are configuration files of the
.Sy runtime
base package.
When the local files have been changed, for example by adding a user, a
port's service account or a group membership,
.Xr pkg 8
cannot always merge a new release into them and leaves the release's
versions beside them as
.Pa /etc/group.pkgnew
and
.Pa /etc/master.passwd.pkgnew .
Groups and users introduced by the release are then missing, and whatever
depends on them fails; for example sound devices are created with group
.Sy audio
.Pq gid 43 .
.Pp
The runtime package installs a pristine copy of the shipped account files in
.Pa /usr/share/base-accounts
and runs
.Nm
after every install and upgrade.
.Nm
compares the local files with that copy and creates each group, then each
user, that is missing by name, with the shipped numeric id, password field,
class, comment, home directory and shell.
It uses
.Xr pw 8
without
.Xr pw.conf 5
defaults, so the account databases are rebuilt and locked exactly as for any
other account change, and no default group memberships are added.
.Pp
.Nm
only adds entries:
.Bl -bullet
.It
An existing local group or user is never modified or removed, even when its
id differs from the shipped one.
Membership of existing groups is never changed; a group created by
.Nm
lists only shipped members that exist.
.It
An entry whose name is missing but whose id is used by a local entry of
another name is refused with a message on standard error; it is not
renumbered, and it is reported again on every run until the administrator
resolves the conflict.
A user whose primary group does not exist is refused in the same way.
.It
Superuser aliases (uid 0, such as
.Sy toor )
and accounts without a password field are never created.
.It
Each shipped name that exists locally, was created or was deliberately
skipped is recorded in
.Pa /var/db/base-accounts ;
refused entries are not.
An entry recorded there that is later missing was removed by the
administrator and is not recreated unless
.Fl a
is given.
.It
The first run has no such record, so it cannot tell an account the
administrator removed from one the installed release never had.
It creates only the missing accounts listed in
.Pa /usr/share/base-accounts/introduced ,
which names each group and user a recent release introduced together with
that release; every other missing shipped account is taken to have been
removed, recorded, and not recreated unless
.Fl a
is given.
.El
.Pp
Running
.Nm
again is harmless.
Creating a group does not grant anyone access: add the people who need a
device group, for example with
.Dl pw groupmod audio -m alice
and log in again.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "-R rootdir"
.It Fl a
Also create entries recorded in
.Pa /var/db/base-accounts
that were removed locally, and on a first run every missing shipped entry.
.It Fl n
Report what would be created without changing anything.
.It Fl q
Do not report created entries.
.It Fl R Ar rootdir
Operate on the system installed under
.Ar rootdir ,
as
.Xr pw 8
.Fl R
does.
.It Fl r Ar refdir
Read the reference
.Pa group
and
.Pa master.passwd
from
.Ar refdir
instead of
.Ar rootdir Ns Pa /usr/share/base-accounts ,
for example
.Pa /usr/src/etc
on a system upgraded from source.
The list of introduced accounts is read from
.Ar refdir
when it has one and from the installed copy otherwise.
.It Fl v
Also report kept, skipped and removed entries.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/usr/share/base-accounts" -compact
.It Pa /usr/share/base-accounts
shipped
.Pa group
and
.Pa master.passwd
reference copies, and
.Pa introduced ,
the accounts each release introduced
.It Pa /var/db/base-accounts
names reconciled by the last run
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 when every shipped account exists or was created or deliberately
skipped, 2 when at least one entry was refused or could not be created, 64
for a usage error and 66 when a reference or account file cannot be read.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Show what an upgraded system is missing:
.Dl base-accounts -nv
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr group 5 ,
.Xr passwd 5 ,
.Xr pkg 8 ,
.Xr pw 8 ,
.Xr pwd_mkdb 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
utility appeared in
.Fx 5BSD .
